<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Bishopric of Samland</h1><p class="st-meta">1243 CE – 1525 CE · capital Fischhausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Prince-bishopric of the Sambian peninsula and Königsberg, whose bishop was the first in Europe to hand his lands to a Protestant duke.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Founded in 1243 as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena's</a> four Prussian dioceses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambia-peninsula">Samland</a> took shape after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sambians">Sambians</a> in 1255. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> received a third of the diocese, with residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primorsk-kaliningrad-oblast">Fischhausen</a> and a cathedral on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kneiphof">Kneiphof</a> island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Like <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pomesanians">Pomesania</a>, its cathedral chapter was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> worked closely with the grand masters. The bishopric's lands included rich amber-gathering coasts, though the amber monopoly belonged to the Order.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-von-polentz">Georg von Polentz</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1523 and in 1525 transferred his secular rule to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Duke Albert</a> when the Order's state became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-prussia">Ducal Prussia</a>, the first Protestant state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambia-peninsula">Samland</a> became part of the duchy, and its lands later lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="prince bishopric, 1243 CE – 1525 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">prince bishopric</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.44%;width:5.61%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1243 CE – 1525 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fischhausen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Georg von Polentz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prince-Bishopric of Samland, Bistum Samland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=bishopric-of-samland" aria-label="Bishopric of Samland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5131 305 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bishopric of Samland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5451 561L5451 561M5451 561L5451 561"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5451 561h0M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5451 561h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=bishopric-of-samland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
